at the Cricket restaurant in Soho London  they prepare for evening service  staff here anxiously awaiting details of  government plans to help businesses with  the skyrocketing cost of energy owner  Rick Campbell is relieved that with  everything else he has to worry about  prime minister Liz truss has ruled out  energy rationing you've got to be  realistic you know a restaurant there's  a lot of high energy things on all the  time we could do dinner by candlelight  but it's not really going to make much  difference to the pills so I don't think  we should just discourage people from  trying to rationalize in some respects  but you have to be realistic  but Russia's invasion of Ukraine and  Vladimir Putin's slow throttling of the  gas supply has changed the calculation  for other countries action is needed  right now we must use less energy in  Ireland the conversation about reducing  consumption has begun  and Germany dependent on Russia for much  of its gas is already in the early  stages of a rationing plan  the UK is less exposed than its European  counterparts but we still have to import  energy in a volatile Global Market and  that leaves us vulnerable  UK officials are still as a matter of  course preparing for a worst case  scenario this winter a combination of  very cold weather and a problem with  Supply now in that scenario the argument  goes without rationing we run the risk  of uncontrolled blackouts  it leaves some experts asking why the  government is so reluctant to ask us to  change our Behavior it makes sense  because the gas that we don't use we  don't have to pay for  um so it brings down bills and the gas  that we don't use makes us more energy  secure in the first place  but for economists the effect on  industry makes rationing a last resort  we will have a reduction in production  potentially they wouldn't be able to  produce as much and that will have  obviously a significant consequences for  for the economy and prioritizing  economic growth is at the heart of Liz  truss's promise to voters one she seems  set on keeping even as an uncertain  winter looms Hannah Thomas Peters Sky  News
 

